{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_stop","index":2},"session_id":"db8806b3-f099-42bb-9304-df6ee5a40e0d","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"9c27a76a-107b-46cc-881e-5df1909b48e2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Aqp7P4kfjd9rhjDrAVZcg8","type":"tool_result","content":"860\t**3.** A StatefulSet's Pods run normally, but the replicas cannot discover each other and the cluster never forms. All Pods are `Running` and `Ready`. What should you check first?\n861\t\n862\t- A) The readiness probe definitions\n863\t- B) The headless Service named by `serviceName`, and whether per-Pod DNS names resolve\n864\t- C) The `port`/`targetPort` pairing on the workload's ClusterIP Service\n865\t- D) The PersistentVolumeClaims for each ordinal\n866\t\n867\t**4.** A bug appears only when the application runs in the cluster. The failing code path reads a value that a mutating admission webhook injects into the Pod. Is a local reproduction useful?\n868\t\n869\t- A) Yes — copy the injected value into a local environment variable and run it locally\n870\t- B) No — the value's origin is the thing in question, and a local copy reproduces your assumption rather than the bug\n871\t- C) Yes — mutating webhooks run identically against local processes\n872\t- D) No — but only because local machines cannot run containers\n873\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db8806b3-f099-42bb-9304-df6ee5a40e0d","uuid":"de248a63-7a79-4eb8-8d8f-99ea64be62ae","timestamp":"2026-08-31T12:15:59.016Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-16\\draft-v1.md","content":"**3.** A StatefulSet's Pods run normally, but the replicas cannot discover each other and the cluster never forms. All Pods are `Running` and `Ready`. What should you check first?\n\n- A) The readiness probe definitions\n- B) The headless Service named by `serviceName`, and whether per-Pod DNS names resolve\n- C) The `port`/`targetPort` pairing on the workload's ClusterIP Service\n- D) The PersistentVolumeClaims for each ordinal\n\n**4.** A bug appears only when the application runs in the cluster. The failing code path reads a value that a mutating admission webhook injects into the Pod. Is a local reproduction useful?\n\n- A) Yes — copy the injected value into a local environment variable and run it locally\n- B) No — the value's origin is the thing in question, and a local copy reproduces your assumption rather than the bug\n- C) Yes — mutating webhooks run identically against local processes\n- D) No — but only because local machines cannot run containers\n","numLines":14,"startLine":860,"totalLines":1224}}}
